It is not a stupid question. I play only the Mac OS X tiles game. I 
started playing it three months ago -- actually, I hadn't heard of 
NetHack until then -- but figured out debug mode just two weeks ago. 
The only way I know to do it: open the NetHack.app package and run the 
contained 'nethack' program from the command line with the appropriate 
arguments. Assuming you haven't modified the source, you also have to 
run the game from a user account named 'wizard'.

For example, if you have NetHack installed in /Applications:

* log on as 'wizard'
* run Terminal
* navigate to /Applications/NetHack.app/Contents/MacOS/
* a directory listing shows the command-line program 'nethack'
* run "nethack -D" or whatever is appropriate for your needs
* the tiles game opens
* the terminal session also stays open, with the tiles games consuming
  keyboard input
* once the tiles game exits, input control returns to the Terminal session

Note that 'wizard' can be a standard user instead of an administrative 
user. I have a very simple 'wizard' user account set up just to play 
NetHack in debug mode. If you have fast user switching activated, you 
can run a normal/real game in your main account, and switch to your 
'wizard' account to check some aspect of game play. Whether you 
consider such parallel gaming ethical is a separate matter. (I use 
'wizard' as a simulator between real games but not during them.)

As a related aside: I've found that setting the NETHACKOPTIONS 
environment variable before running 'nethack' in Terminal offers game 
control that the tiles version doesn't seem to allow otherwise. There 
are several options that I've tried setting in '~/Preferences/Nethack 
Defaults' that the game won't honor; but if I set those same options in 
the NETHACKOPTIONS environment, the game honors them. I now run all 
NetHack sessions, including non-debug, from the command line for this 
very reason.

Running this way has the added benefit of letting you separate the 
NetHack playground from the NetHack.app package. Such separation helps 
prevent osteoporosis during NetHack reinstallation/update. It also 
overcomes what I think is a dubious coupling of program implementation 
and program state. (I'm reminded of 20+ years ago, when my computer 
literacy students would store each document in the creating 
application's folder.) This is still an incomplete solution, as I'd 
argue (perhaps from ignorance) that the save/bones files should be 
completely separate from the installed level files, et al; but pointing 
'nethackdir' outside the package is a good easy compromise.


